  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your roof insurance claim was denied, you do not have to accept the decision as final, as many denials can be successfully challenged or reversed.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "you do not have to accept the decision as final"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "1. Read the Denial Letter Closely"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Find the exact reason: Look for why the insurer said no (e.g., normal wear and tear, pre-existing damage, cosmetic-only damage, or missed deadlines).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Review your policy: Check your coverage details, exclusions, and whether your policy pays out on an Actual Cash Value (ACV) or Replacement Cost Value (RCV) basis.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "2. Get an Independent Second Opinion"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Hire a licensed roofer: Have a reputable, independent local roofing contractor inspect your roof. Ask them to walk all the slopes, mark damage (like hail hits or wind tears) with chalk, and take detailed, timestamped photos and videos.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Compare scopes side-by-side: Line up the insurance company\u2019s damage estimate against your contractor\u2019s report to see what was missed, ignored, or misclassified.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Gather weather data: Pull certified historical storm or hail reports for your address on the date the damage supposedly happened.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "3. Request a Re-Inspection"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Submit a written request: Ask your insurance company in writing for a re-inspection."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Have your roofer present: Make sure your independent roofing contractor or a hired expert is on-site when the insurance adjuster or a second adjuster comes out to point out the evidence.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "4. File a Formal Appeal or Supplement"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Write a rebuttal: Submit a formal appeal letter with a point-by-point response to the denial reason, backed by your contractor's photos, repair records, and weather reports. Do this within your policy's timeline (often 30 to 60 days).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "5. Explore Further Escalation Options"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If the insurer still refuses to budge, you have deeper recourse:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Invoke the Appraisal Clause: If you and the insurance company agree that damage exists but disagree heavily on the dollar amount, check your policy for an appraisal clause to hire independent appraisers.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hire a Public Adjuster: A licensed public adjuster can independently evaluate and document the damage for a small percentage (usually 10%\u201315%) of the claim payout. (Note: On , users note that public adjusters charge fees and final coverage decisions are typically made by desk adjusters rather than field inspectors alone).",
            "snippet_links": [
              {
                "link": "https://www.reddit.com/r/Insurance/comments/1busr46/insurance_denied_my_roof_claim_but_it_makes_zero/"
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              7,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Contact your State Insurance Department: File a formal complaint if you believe the insurer acted in bad faith or ignored valid proof.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Consult an Insurance Attorney: An attorney specializing in property claims can review your contract and challenge wrongful denials.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              10,
              11
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down your next steps, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What specific reason did the denial letter give?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Do you have photos or an independent report from a contractor yet?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
